<http://data.silknow.org/object/fe250d17-2d4d-3e25-b0fc-f75cf543cb31/observation/3>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Old label:\n\nThis 17th century Persian vase carpet contains all the elements of the larger one displayed on the wall to the right, but in a more stylised form. The large, imaginary blossoms have been placed on the lines of three superimposed lattices, a white one, a dark one and a light blue one; stiff sprays of flowers have been placed in the spaces between the lines. A vase, after which this type of carpet is named, is in the centre. []"@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/fe250d17-2d4d-3e25-b0fc-f75cf543cb31> .

<http://data.silknow.org/production/a85e9fad-0956-3e9a-9ddd-25b117cd1c08>
        a                       ecrm:E12_Production ;
        ecrm:P108_has_produced  <http://data.silknow.org/object/fe250d17-2d4d-3e25-b0fc-f75cf543cb31> ;
        ecrm:P126_employed      "Technical Analysis:\nWarp:  white cotton, Z4S, depressed.  32 per inch.\nWeft:  white sinuous silk and white cotton (presumed - colour not visible) pulled straight.\nSilk: 2-spun, , 3 shoots per row of knots: silk, cotton, silk\n15 rows of knots per inch\n\nPile:  wool, 18 colours:  red, orange, dark yellow, yellow, dark green, green, very light green, dark blue, blue, light blue, very light blue, dark purple, purple, dark pink, pink, light pink, dark brown, white.  Asymmetrical, open to the left, tied round 2 warp threads.\nSide finish:  not original\nEnd finish:  missing\n\n(analysed through glass 19.5.99  MV & FA)"@en ;
        ecrm:P126_employed      <http://data.silknow.org/vocabulary/900> , <http://data.silknow.org/vocabulary/617> ;
        ecrm:P4_has_time-span   <http://data.silknow.org/timespan/1650_1700> ;
        ecrm:P8_took_place_on_or_within
                <https://sws.geonames.org/130758/> .

<http://data.silknow.org/event/bbabf800-56d4-3409-940e-fb0369d07772>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Purchased on behalf of the Museum from Edgar and Alice Whitaker, executors for the Istanbul estate of William Henry Wrench (1836-96), British Consul to Ottoman Turkey. Caspar Purdon Clarke, then Director of the Art Museum at South Kensington, travelled to Istanbul to negotiate a bulk purchase from Wrench's well-known art collection. He singled out this carpet in his correspondence, noting that \"the Ispahan carpet [...] was well known to amateurs, and Mr Wrench had refused £300 for it, indeed all offers to purchase\". He noted that Wrench had purchased the carpet ten years previously for £85. In the printed catalogue of the Wrench sale, the carpet was described as an \"Ispahan portière\" [door curtain]. Indeed the carpet may be seen hanging on an interior wall (presumably covering a doorway) of Wrench's home in Istanbul, in an archive photograph captioned \"Constantinople. The Wrench Collection at Pera. Room showing Persian art objects\" (V&A Archives). A second carpet (V&A 358-1897) may be seen lying on the floor in the same photograph."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/fe250d17-2d4d-3e25-b0fc-f75cf543cb31> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Purchased on behalf of the Museum from Edgar and Alice Whitaker, executors for the Istanbul estate of William Henry Wrench (1836-96), British Consul to Ottoman Turkey. Caspar Purdon Clarke, then Director of the Art Museum at South Kensington, travelled to Istanbul to negotiate a bulk purchase from Wrench's well-known art collection. He singled out this carpet in his correspondence, noting that \"the Ispahan carpet [...] was well known to amateurs, and Mr Wrench had refused £300 for it, indeed all offers to purchase\". He noted that Wrench had purchased the carpet ten years previously for £85. In the printed catalogue of the Wrench sale, the carpet was described as an \"Ispahan portière\" [door curtain]. Indeed the carpet may be seen hanging on an interior wall (presumably covering a doorway) of Wrench's home in Istanbul, in an archive photograph captioned \"Constantinople. The Wrench Collection at Pera. Room showing Persian art objects\" (V&A Archives). A second carpet (V&A 358-1897) may be seen lying on the floor in the same photograph."@en .

